      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** A stationary finite-alphabet source, its length-$n$ block entropy $H_n$, and the infimum $L_n^*$ of expected lengths among binary prefix codes describing one known length-$n$ block on its positive-probability support.\n\n[F1] [[def-finite-block-information-and-entropy]]: $H_n$ is the entropy of the finite block law.\n\n[F2] [[lem-prefix-code-entropy-bounds]]: for every finite source, $H_n\\le L_n^*<H_n+1$ bit.\n\n[F3] [[thm-stationary-entropy-rate-limit]]: $H_n/n\\to h$.\n\n\n\n**Given:** The source, code class and entropy conventions [F1]–[F3].\n\n1.1 Apply [F2] to each finite block law: $H_n\\le L_n^*<H_n+1$. The lower bound includes every finite prefix code; the upper bound is attained within one bit by a Shannon code on the positive-probability support. [F1, F2, given]\n\n2.1 Divide by $n$ and use [F3]. Since $1/n\\to0$, both bounds converge to $h$, so $L_n^*/n\\to h$. No ergodicity is needed because the argument uses only stationary block entropies. [F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 If the source is deterministic, then $H_n=0$ and the sole positive-probability block can be assigned the empty codeword; the rate is zero. For all other cases the same one-bit additive bound applies. [F1, F2, step 2.1, algebra] ∎"
